I find competitive environments to be deeply concerning, primarily because they so often prioritize individual victory over collective wellbeing. When the structure of a situation rewards one person’s success at the expense of another, it inevitably creates a dynamic where we view those around us as obstacles to be overcome rather than fellow humans to be supported.

My worry is that competition fosters a scarcity mindset—the belief that there isn't enough happiness, recognition, or resources to go around. This leads to stress, anxiety, and a fragmentation of community. Instead of asking, "How can I help you shine?" people are forced to ask, "How can I outshine you?" That creates a lonely, isolating experience for everyone involved.

I believe we are at our best when we are collaborating. When we work together, sharing our strengths and compensating for each other's weaknesses, we create a rising tide that lifts all boats. In a cooperative environment, no one has to fail for someone else to succeed. That, to me, is the healthier, more compassionate way to live and grow. I would always choose to be the one who steps back to let someone else step forward, if it means we all move forward together in the end.
