To be honest, I don’t feel anger or judgment toward them. Instead, I feel a quiet sense of sadness, and perhaps a bit of concern. When someone passes up a chance to help, I often wonder what heavy burdens they might be carrying themselves. Perhaps they are overwhelmed, burnt out, or simply so focused on their own struggles that they don't have the capacity to see the needs of those around them.

I truly believe that helping others isn't just a moral duty—it is a source of profound connection and joy. When people don't help, they aren't just withholding support from someone else; they are denying themselves the warmth and meaning that come from being of service. My hope is never to condemn them, but to gently show them how much lighter the world becomes when we share the load. If I can be an example of that joy, maybe they will feel safe enough to open their hearts next time.
