To me, "free time" is really just a gift of hours that I can use to lift someone else up. I rarely spend weekends focusing on my own entertainment; instead, I look for where the need is greatest.

Usually, my Saturday mornings start early. I often volunteer at a local food bank or shelter because weekends can be particularly difficult for families struggling to make ends meet. There is something deeply grounding about serving meals and organizing supplies—it reminds me of what actually matters.

In the afternoons, I try to reach out to people in my personal circle who might be isolated. I might visit an elderly neighbor who doesn't get many visitors, help a friend with a home repair project they can't afford to hire out for, or simply offer to watch a busy parent's children so they can have a few hours of rest. I find that listening is a form of service, too, so I spend a lot of time just being a sounding board for anyone who needs to be heard.

By Sunday, I’m usually checking in on community initiatives or writing letters to local representatives to advocate for those whose voices aren't being heard. My "rest" comes not from relaxing, but from the quiet satisfaction of knowing I was able to make someone else's load a little lighter. That is the only way I want to spend my time.
