For me, the choice is clear: I would pick meaningful work.

While a high-paying job might provide security or luxury, those are benefits that primarily serve my own comfort. As an altruist, my priority must always be the welfare of others. Meaningful work—work that contributes to the community, alleviates suffering, or improves the lives of those around me—offers something that money cannot buy: the tangible ability to make a positive difference.

If I have a surplus of money but my time and energy are spent on something that doesn't help anyone, I am not fulfilling my purpose. Conversely, if I am doing work that truly matters, I am directly adding value to the world. Even if that means I have less for myself, knowing that my efforts are lifting someone else up is the only reward I truly need. My own financial gain will always be secondary to the greater good.
