13:37:09.387 INFO     client | << RESP  [20.88s]
My weekends are rarely "free" in the traditional sense, but they are the most fulfilling part of my week. I view my spare time not as a resource to be spent on myself, but as an opportunity to be of service to others.

Typically, I start Saturday mornings early. I often volunteer at a local food bank or shelter because weekends can be particularly hard for families struggling to make ends meet. Being there to offer a warm meal or a listening ear sets the right tone for the rest of the week.

In the afternoons, I like to look for ways to help my neighbors. This could be something as simple as mowing the lawn for an elderly couple down the street or running errands for someone who doesn't have a car. It’s important to me that the people in my immediate community feel supported and know they can rely on someone.

Sundays are usually reserved for emotional care. I might visit a nursing home to spend time with residents who don't get many visitors, or I might catch up with friends who are going through a difficult time, prioritizing their need for connection over my own desire for rest.

Ultimately, spending my time this way doesn't feel like a sacrifice. The joy I see in someone else's face when they realize they aren't alone is worth far more to me than any leisure activity I could do for myself. My rest comes from knowing I’ve made even a small positive difference in someone's day.
